...
21:48:49 INFO drawn n=5 of=843 documents=[docaJSlsHLzhsC docRTQwV0d003o]
```
That's about 15s a page, almost all spent waiting for one sheet write to flush. It adds up to ~25 PDFs an hour, and ~1½ days for the 854 documents left.

- (*) Drop the queue waits
  The uploader sends each PDF's pages back to back. It waits only for its own first page's server thumbnail, which it has to copy onto the PDF. Copying pages between identical PDFs no longer waits either. The server's shared CPU budget keeps people's requests ahead of the thumbnail and search backlog, and queued sheet writes go out together in a few calls per flush (`docs/storage.md`). For `docaJSlsHLzhsC`, I'd expect 13 uploads at about 1–2s each, then a short wait for one thumbnail. That's an estimate; I haven't measured it. Risk: thumbnails and search entries pile up on the server while the run is going, hundreds at a time instead of one. The health monitor would show that, and wake me if a queue stops moving or the API slows past 3s.
- ( ) Keep the waits
  Start it as it is. About 15s a page, roughly 1½ days to finish, and the server never holds more than one page's work.

