### The catch-up you started is running an older build of the uploader. Restart it on the current one?

**Where it stands.** You started `go run ./local/pdf-catchup` in the second Shell terminal at 18:08. Go compiled the uploader at that moment, before my last two fixes landed at 18:14 and 18:26. It's healthy and moving:
- The health monitor shows reads at 72–107 ms and queues near 0.
- Right now it is copying a 35-page PDF onto the 7 other documents that hold the same bytes. 3 of 7 are done. Each copy is one 36-row write that the server runs at background priority (import-mode writes yield to people's requests), so it takes 40–90s. Production logs show writes finishing at 18:21:34, 18:22:16, 18:23:45 and 18:25:08.

**What the running build lacks, compared with the current one:**

| Case | Running build (18:08) | Current build |
|---|---|---|
| Before starting the next PDF | waits only for page 1's server thumbnail, so later pages' thumbnails can pile up on the server PDF after PDF | waits until every page it uploaded has a server thumbnail, so at most one PDF's pages are queued |
| A page the server marked thumbnailed with no picture | the whole run stops | skips that PDF and goes on |
| Copying pages onto a second document of the same PDF | no log line until the whole group finishes. For this 8-document group that's ~10 silent minutes, and a bigger group could trip the wrapper's 20-minute "stalled" alert | logs `reused pdf pages` for each document copied |

**Restarting is safe.** The next pass reads what's already complete from production. Finished documents are skipped, and pages already uploaded are re-sent as no-op duplicates.

